  "generated_text": "To determine the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\), we first need to identify the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 3. Let's calculate the first few powers of 3:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^1 &= 3 &\\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^2 &= 9 &\\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^3 &= 27 &\\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^4 &= 81 &\\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n3^5 &= 243 &\\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^6 &= 729 &\\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^7 &= 2187 &\\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^8 &= 6561 &\\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe observe that the units digits repeat every 4 terms: 3, 9, 7, 1. Therefore, the units digits of \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots\\) form the repeating sequence 3, 7, 3, 7, \\ldots.\n\nNext, we need to count the number of terms in the sequence \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, \\ldots, 3^{2009}\\). This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term \\(a = 1\\) and common difference \\(d = 2\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence is given by:\n\n\\[\na_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 = 2n - 1\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the largest \\(n\\) such that \\(2n - 1 \\leq 2009\\):\n\n\\[\n2n - 1 \\leq 2009 \\implies 2n \\leq 2010 \\implies n \\leq 1005\n\\]\n\nThus, there are 1005 terms in the sequence. Since the units digits repeat every 4 terms, we can determine the number of complete cycles of 4 terms and the remaining terms:\n\n\\[\n1005 \\div 4 = 251 \\text{ remainder } 1\n\\]\n\nThis means there are 251 complete cycles of 3, 7, 3, 7 and 1 additional term (which is 3). The units digits of these 251 cycles are:\n\n\\[\n251 \\times 3 + 3 = 753 + 3 = 756\n\\]\n\nThe units digit of 756 is 6. Adding the units digit of the remaining term (which is 3):\n\n\\[\n6 + 3 = 9\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
